\f0\fs24 \cf0 \\begin\{abstract\}\
Large reasoning models have recently demonstrated emergent \'93aha moments\'94 that enable reflective reasoning. However, a non-trivial portion of reflective steps are ``low-utility,'' where reasoning errors are not corrected, or even correct reasoning trajectories are shifted toward incorrect ones.\
To address low-utility reflective continuation, this paper proposes \\textbf\{\\textsc\{RUAR\}\}, a utility-guided advantage rescaling method for reflective reasoning. During reinforcement learning (RL), \
\\textsc\{RUAR\} rescales the token-level advantages within each reflective reasoning step according to the estimated utility of the reflective continuation. The utility is estimated through \\textbf\{answer-forcing utility estimation\}, which measures how the model\'92s correct-answer probability changes before and after the reflective step by forcing answer generation from both reasoning states using answer-forcing prompts. Furthermore, \\textsc\{RUAR\} introduces an \\textbf\{overthinking-mitigating\
rescaling mechanism\}, where utility-guided rescaling is no longer applied from the earliest\
answer-ready reflection step onward.\
Experiments demonstrate that \\textsc\{RUAR\} improves the accuracy--efficiency trade-off, substantially reducing generated token usage while often preserving or improving final-answer accuracy.\
 \\section\{Introduction\}\
\\label\{sec:introduction\}\
\
\
\\begin\{figure\}[t]\
\\centering\
\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]\{acl-style-files-master/images/intro_v7.pdf\}\
\\caption\{\
