As discussed in Section~\\ref\{sec:introduction\}, there is no guarantee that reflective reasoning steps consistently improve answer correctness throughout the reasoning trajectory. This motivates estimating the utility of each reflective step based on its before/after effect on correct-answer probability, rather than assigning static rewards or penalties solely based on the presence of reflective tokens.\
\
\\subsection\{Length-Regularized RLOO Trainer\}\
\\label\{subsec:rloo_backbone\}\
\
For each prompt $x$, we sample $N$ rollouts $\\\{\\bm\{y\}^\{(i)\}\\\}_\{i=1\}^\{N\}$ from the\
current policy and assign each rollout a terminal reward $R_i$. Our base trainer\
uses a REINFORCE leave-one-out (RLOO) advantage estimator~\\citep\{ahmadian-etal-2024-back\}. For rollout $i$, the\
leave-one-out baseline and rollout-level advantage are\
\\[\
\\bar\{R\}_\{-i\}\
=\
\\frac\{1\}\{N-1\}\
\\sum_\{n\\ne i\} R_n,\
\\qquad\
\\hat\{A\}_i\
=\
R_i-\\bar\{R\}_\{-i\}.\
\\]\
This rollout-level signal is broadcast to response tokens, yielding the base\
token-level advantage signal $A_\{i,t\}$ that \\textsc\{RUAR\} later rescales before\
the final policy update.\
\
Following prior efficient-reasoning work~\\citep\{arora2026training\}, we use a\
length-regularized terminal reward in the RLOO trainer. The exact reward\
definition is provided in Appendix~\\ref\{app:length_regularized_reward\}.\
\
\
