\\section\{Method\}\
\\label\{sec:method\}\
\
\
\\subsection\{Overview\}\
\
As discussed in Section~\\ref\{subsec:rloo_backbone\}, standard outcome-supervised\
RL assigns the same trace-level supervision signal to a full response by using\
identical advantage scores across all tokens in a reasoning trace, without\
considering that reflective reasoning steps may differ substantially in whether\
they correct, repeat, or derail the solution trajectory. Instead of using\
trace-constant advantages $A_\{i,t\}$, \\textsc\{RUAR\} estimates the utility of\
each reflective reasoning step $\\bm\{s\}_j$ from its before/after effect on\
correct-answer probability under answer-forcing prompts, and uses this utility\
to reshape token-level advantage signals during RL training. \
\
In addition, to mitigate\
overthinking, \\textsc\{RUAR\} further identifies the \\textit\{earliest answer-ready step\}:\
before this step, it applies utility-guided rescaling to reflective reasoning\
steps, while after this step, it applies fixed overthinking-mitigating\
rescaling to redundant post-ready continuation. For readability, we use simplified notation for step-level quantities within a\
fixed rollout, while Algorithm~\\ref\{alg:RUAR\} uses rollout-indexed notation for\
the full training procedure.\
\
\\subsection\{Answer-Forcing Utility Estimation\}\
\\label\{subsec:answer_forcing_utility\}\
\
With multiple reflective reasoning steps defined in\
Section~\\ref\{subsec:reflection_steps\}, \\textsc\{RUAR\} performs\
answer-forcing utility estimation to determine whether each reflective\
step improves correct-answer probability. For the $j$-th\
reflective reasoning step $\\bm\{s\}_j$ in $\\bm\{y\}$, defined as\
$\\bm\{s\}_j = y_\{a_j\}, \\cdots, y_\{e_j\}$, we define the corresponding \\textit\{before-step\} and \\textit\{after-step\}\
partial traces as\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\bm\{s\}_j^- = \\bm\{y\}_\{1:a_j-1\},\
\\qquad\
\\bm\{s\}_j^+ = \\bm\{y\}_\{1:e_j\}.\
\\label\{eq_before_after_partial_trace\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
Here, $\\bm\{y\}_\{m:n\}$ denotes the span of response tokens starting from $y_m$\
and ending at $y_n$.\
\
%Given a before-step or after-step partial trace $\\bm\{y\}_\{1:k\}$ (i.e., either $\\bm\{s\}_j^-$ or $\\bm\{s\}_j^+$), we apply an \\textit\{answer-forcing prompt\} $\{\\mathcal\{A\}\}$ to evaluate whether $\\bm\{y\}_\{1:k\}$ is sufficient for the model $\\pi_\\theta$ to generate the correct final answer when explicitly forced to answer, as follows:\
Given a before-step or after-step partial trace\
$\\bm\{y\}_\{1:q\}$ (i.e., either $\\bm\{s\}_j^-$ or $\\bm\{s\}_j^+$), we apply an\
\\textit\{answer-forcing prompt\} $\{\\mathcal\{A\}\}$ to evaluate whether\
$\\bm\{y\}_\{1:q\}$ contains sufficient reasoning information for the model\
$\\pi_\\theta$ to generate the correct final answer when explicitly forced\
to answer, as follows:\
%. Formally, let $\{\\mathcal\{A\}\}$ denote the appended answer-forcing prompt:\
\\begin\{tcolorbox\}[\
    colback=gray!10,\
    colframe=gray!10,\
    arc=3mm,\
    boxrule=0pt,\
    halign=center\
]\
\\texttt\{\\textbackslash n**Final Answer**\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash boxed\}\
\\end\{tcolorbox\}\
\
We then sample $K$ short \\textit\{final-answer continuations\} from the current policy. Let\
$o_\{j,k\}^-$ and $o_\{j,k\}^+$ denote the $k$-th continuations sampled from\
$\\bm\{s\}_j^- \\oplus \{\\mathcal\{A\}\}$ and $\\bm\{s\}_j^+ \\oplus \{\\mathcal\{A\}\}$,\
respectively, where $\\oplus$ denotes string concatenation. Let\
$\\textsf\{correct\}$ be a \\textit\{rule-based final-answer verifier\} satisfying\
$\\textsf\{correct\}(\\cdot) \\in \\\{0,1\\\}$. The estimated correct-answer\
probabilities for the before-step and after-step partial traces are computed as\
follows:\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
p_j^- &=\
\\frac\{1\}\{K\}\\sum_\{k=1\}^\{K\}\
\\textsf\{correct\}(x,\\bm\{s\}_j^- \\oplus \{\\mathcal\{A\}\} \\oplus o_\{j,k\}^-),\\\\\
p_j^+ &=\
\\frac\{1\}\{K\}\\sum_\{k=1\}^\{K\}\
\\textsf\{correct\}(x,\\bm\{s\}_j^+ \\oplus \{\\mathcal\{A\}\} \\oplus o_\{j,k\}^+).\
\\end\{aligned\}\
\\label\{eq_before_after_correct_answer_probabilities\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
where $p_j^-$ and $p_j^+$ denote the \\textit\{before-step\} and\
\\textit\{after-step correct-answer probabilities\}, respectively.\
\
The utility of the reflective reasoning step $\\bm\{s\}_j$ is then defined as\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
u_j = p_j^+ - p_j^- .\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
The verifier $\\textsf\{correct\}$ evaluates only the sampled final-answer\
continuations and does not provide human process labels or step-level\
supervision. \
See Appendix~\\ref\{app:answer_probe_details\} for implementation\
details of the answer-forcing prompt and verification procedure.\
\
\
\
\\subsection\{Utility-Guided Advantage Rescaling\}\
\\label\{subsec:utility_rescaling\}\
\
Let $A_\{i,t\}$ denote the base token-level advantage signal assigned by the\
length-regularized RLOO trainer before the final policy update. \\textsc\{RUAR\}\
constructs a token-level scaling factor $\\gamma_\{i,t\}$ and computes the\
rescaled advantage signal as follows:\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
A'_\{i,t\} = \\gamma_\{i,t\} A_\{i,t\}.\
\\label\{eq_advantage_rescaling\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
The RL trainer subsequently applies its standard advantage normalization\
procedure to $A'_\{i,t\}$ before the PPO-style policy update.\
\
For a token $t$ belonging to a reflective reasoning step $\\bm\{s\}_j$ to which\
utility-guided rescaling is applied, \\textsc\{RUAR\} computes the scaling factor\
based on the estimated utility $u_j$ of the reflective step:\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\gamma_\{i,t\} =\
\\begin\{cases\}\
\\max(0,1+u_j), & A_\{i,t\}\\ge 0,\\\\\
\\max(0,1-u_j), & A_\{i,t\}<0.\
\\end\{cases\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
Thus, when $A_\{i,t\}$ is positive, helpful reflective steps receive stronger\
positive credit, while harmful reflective steps receive weaker positive credit.\
Conversely, when $A_\{i,t\}$ is negative, helpful reflective steps are penalized\
less, whereas harmful reflective steps are penalized more strongly.\
\
\
\\subsection\{Overthinking-Mitigating Rescaling\}\
\\label\{subsec:overthinking_rescaling\}\
\
The utility-guided rescaling rule in\
Section~\\ref\{subsec:utility_rescaling\} assigns step-specific credit according\
to estimated step utility. However, once a reasoning trajectory is already\
likely to produce the correct final answer, additional reflective continuation\
can become redundant. \\textsc\{RUAR\} therefore applies utility-guided rescaling\
only before the model becomes answer-ready, and switches to fixed post-ready\
rescaling afterward.\
\
\\subsubsection\{Pre-Ready and Post-Ready Reflection\}\
\
We divide reflective reasoning into two stages: pre-ready and post-ready.\
\
1) \\textbf\{Pre-ready stage.\}\
At the pre-ready stage, the current reasoning trajectory is not yet sufficiently\
reliable, and further exploration or self-correction may still be required\
before the model becomes ready to generate the correct answer.\
\
2) \\textbf\{Post-ready stage.\}\
At the post-ready stage, the reasoning trajectory has become sufficiently\
reliable, and the model is already likely to generate the correct answer.\
Additional reflective continuation after this point is therefore more likely to\
become redundant and contribute to overthinking behavior.\
\
To identify the transition between these stages, we define a reflective step as\
\\textit\{near answer-ready\} if its before-step correct-answer probability is at\
least a threshold $\\tau$. If the $j$-th reflective step and its following\
$c-1$ consecutive reflective steps are all near answer-ready, then the $j$-th\
step is identified as the earliest answer-ready reflection step, referred to\
simply as the \\textit\{answer-ready step\}. Formally, the index $b$ of the\
answer-ready step is\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
b = \\min \\\{j : \\;& p_r^- \\ge \\tau \\\\\
&\\text\{for \} r=j,\\ldots,j+c-1\\\}.\
\\end\{aligned\}\
\\label\{eq:ready_boundary\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
If no such consecutive run exists, we set $b=m+1$.\
\
Using the answer-ready step $\\bm\{s\}_b$, we define pre-ready and post-ready\
reflective reasoning steps as follows:\
1) \\textbf\{Pre-ready reflective steps\}:\
$\
\\bm\{y\}^\{\\mathrm\{pre\}\}\
=\
\\left(\
\\bm\{s\}_1,\\cdots,\\bm\{s\}_\{b-1\}\
\\right)$. 2) \\textbf\{Post-ready reflective steps\}: \
$\\bm\{y\}^\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}\
=\
\\left(\
\\bm\{s\}_\{b\},\\cdots,\\bm\{s\}_\{m\}\
\\right).\
$\
\
Here, $\\bm\{y\}^\{\\mathrm\{pre\}\}$ contains reflective steps before the\
answer-ready step, while $\\bm\{y\}^\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}$ contains the answer-ready\
step itself and later reflective continuation, i.e., from the answer-ready\
step onward, inclusive. During training, once the answer-ready step is\
detected, \\textsc\{RUAR\} stops computing additional answer-forcing utility\
estimates for the remaining reflective continuation within the rollout. A\
concrete probing example is provided in Appendix~\\ref\{app:case_step_utility\},\
while Appendix~\\ref\{app:ready_boundary_diagnostics\} further analyzes the\
behavior of answer-ready step detection.\
\
\
\\subsubsection\{Pre-Ready and Post-Ready Rescaling\}\
\
\
Using the pre-ready and post-ready reflective reasoning steps defined above,\
\\textsc\{RUAR\} specifies which scaling factor $\\gamma_\{i,t\}$ is used in\
Eq.~\\ref\{eq_advantage_rescaling\} for each token region. For pre-ready tokens\
$y_t \\in \\bm\{y\}^\{\\mathrm\{pre\}\}$, the scaling factor $\\gamma_\{i,t\}$ follows the\
utility-guided rule in Section~\\ref\{subsec:utility_rescaling\}. For post-ready\
continuation, \\textsc\{RUAR\} no longer uses the estimated step utility. Once the\
answer-ready step $\\bm\{s\}_b$ is detected, fixed post-ready rescaling is applied\
to all remaining reasoning-segment tokens from the start of $\\bm\{s\}_b$ onward:\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\gamma_\{i,t\} =\
\\begin\{cases\}\
\\gamma_\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}^+, & A_\{i,t\}\\ge 0,\\\\\
\\gamma_\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}^-, & A_\{i,t\}<0,\
\\end\{cases\}\
\\label\{eq_pre_ready_post_ready_rescaling\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
where $\\gamma_\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}^+ < 1$ and $\\gamma_\{\\mathrm\{post\}\}^- > 1$.\
%This formulation reduces positive credit for reflective continuation after the model has already become answer-ready, while increasing the penalty when such continuation appears under negative advantage signals.\
\
Tokens outside both pre-ready reflective reasoning steps and this post-ready\
continuation use the default scaling factor $\\gamma_\{i,t\}=1$.\\footnote\{\
In the main setting, \\textit\{Alternatively\}-start continuations before the\
answer-ready step are not selected as target reflective reasoning steps for\
utility-guided rescaling, although \\textit\{Alternatively\} can terminate a\
preceding \\textit\{Wait\}-start step. See\
Appendix~\\ref\{app:case_endpoint_delimitation\} for a concrete example.\
\}\
\
\
\
\\subsection\{Policy Optimization\}\
\
After standard advantage normalization, we use the normalized\
\\textsc\{RUAR\}-adjusted advantage signal in the same PPO-style policy objective\
as the base trainer:\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\begin\{aligned\}\
\\mathcal\{L\}_\{\\mathrm\{policy\}\}\
= -\\mathbb\{E\}_\{i,t\}\\bigl[\\min(&r_\{i,t\}A'_\{i,t\},\\\\\
&\\operatorname\{clip\}_\{\\epsilon\}(r_\{i,t\})A'_\{i,t\})\\bigr].\
\\end\{aligned\}\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
where\
\
\\begin\{equation\}\
\\operatorname\{clip\}_\{\\epsilon\}(r)\
=\
\\operatorname\{clip\}(r,1-\\epsilon,1+\\epsilon).\
\\end\{equation\}\
\
Here, $r_\{i,t\}$ denotes the policy likelihood ratio. \\textsc\{RUAR\} preserves\
the original outcome-based RL trainer, including rollout generation, terminal\
outcome scoring, and terminal reward computation. The additional component introduced by \\textsc\{RUAR\} is a step-level rescaling\
layer that applies utility-guided scaling before the answer-ready step and fixed\
post-ready scaling afterward.\
\
\
