\\subsection\{Experimental Setup\}\
\\label\{sec:experimental-setup\}\
\
\\paragraph\{Backbones and baselines.\}\
We evaluate \\textsc\{RUAR\} as a post-training method for improving the\
accuracy--efficiency trade-off of reasoning models. Our main comparison spans\
two open-weight reasoning backbones: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B~\\citep\{Guo_2025\}\
and Qwen3-8B~\\citep\{yang2025qwen3technicalreport\}.  For each backbone, we compare four policies: the original Base model,\
the length-regularized RL baseline of \\citet\{arora2026training\}, denoted\
TLMRE, PRIME~\\citep\{cui2026process\}, and \\textsc\{RUAR\}. TLMRE serves as the direct length-efficiency baseline for \\textsc\{RUAR\}, while\
PRIME is included as a process-signal baseline that, like \\textsc\{RUAR\}, avoids\
human process labels but uses implicit reward modeling rather than\
reflective-step utility rescaling.\
\
\\paragraph\{Training and checkpoint selection.\}\
The \\textsc\{RUAR\} policies are trained with online outcome-supervised RL on\
Numina-3K, a subset of NuminaMath-CoT~\\citep\{numina_math_datasets\}. NuminaMath-CoT\
contains chain-of-thought math problem-solution pairs from diverse sources,\
including MATH, Olympiad problems, and AMC/AIME. For each prompt, we sample 16 rollouts and compute base advantages\
using the RLOO estimator~\\citep\{ahmadian-etal-2024-back\} before applying \\textsc\{RUAR\}'s step-level rescaling. We train DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B for 150 steps and Qwen3-8B\
for 50 steps, and select checkpoints by the validation accuracy--length\
trade-off. For a fair comparison under the same training-step budget, TLMRE and PRIME are reported at the same\
training step as the selected RUAR checkpoint for each backbone. Full training budgets and baseline matching\
details are provided in Appendix~\\ref\{app:training-details\}, with additional\
training dynamics and cost analyses in Appendix~\\ref\{app:additional_training_analysis\}.\
\
\\paragraph\{Benchmarks.\}\
Our main table reports results on mathematical reasoning and\
held-out multiple-choice reasoning benchmarks. The mathematical group\
contains GSM8K~\\citep\{cobbe2021trainingverifierssolvemath\},\
MATH500~\\citep\{lightman2024lets\}, AIME2024~\\citep\{jia2024aime\}, and\
HMMT2025~\\citep\{matharena2025hmmtfeb\}. Since training uses Numina-3K, we also\
evaluate out-of-domain generalization on non-math benchmarks:\
GPQA-Diamond~\\citep\{rein2023gpqagraduatelevelgoogleproofqa\},\
ARC-Challenge~\\citep\{clark2018thinksolvedquestionanswering\}, and\
CommonsenseQA~\\citep\{talmor-etal-2019-commonsenseqa\}. We additionally verify\
that the Numina-3K training split has no direct problem-statement overlap with\
the main evaluation benchmarks; details are provided in\
Appendix~\\ref\{app:decontamination\}.\
\
\\paragraph\{Decoding and metrics.\}\
To isolate policy differences without adding sampling variance, all main-table\
results use greedy single-sample decoding for all methods, with one response per\
problem and a maximum response length of 32768 tokens. We report accuracy,\
average generated response length in tokens, and the accuracy-efficiency score\
(AES) from O1-Pruner~\\citep\{luo2025opruner\}, computed relative to the Base row. Full decoding, scoring, and AES computation\
details are provided in Appendix~\\ref\{app:evaluation-details\}.\
\
\\subsection\{Main Result\}\
\\label\{sec:main-result\}\
\
Table~\\ref\{tab:main-eval\} shows that \\textsc\{RUAR\} improves the\
accuracy--efficiency trade-off in most settings across model scales and\
benchmark types. Across two backbones and seven benchmarks, \\textsc\{RUAR\}\
achieves the best or tied-best AES in 13 out of 14 settings and the shortest\
average response length in 13 out of 14 settings.\
\
On mathematical reasoning benchmarks, \\textsc\{RUAR\} substantially reduces\
generated tokens while often preserving or improving accuracy. For the 1.5B\
model, it improves accuracy across all math datasets while producing much\
shorter responses. For Qwen3-8B, it reduces tokens by up to roughly 60\\%,\
matching or improving Base accuracy on GSM8K and AIME2024, with small drops\
on MATH500 and HMMT2025.\
\
The trade-off gains also transfer to held-out non-math benchmarks:\
\\textsc\{RUAR\} improves non-math accuracy for the 1.5B model and produces\
substantially shorter responses for both backbones. Compared with TLMRE,\
these results suggest benefits beyond length-only compression; compared with\
PRIME, which applies implicit token-level process rewards across the entire\
response, \\textsc\{RUAR\}'s selective utility-based rescaling over reflective\
reasoning steps yields a stronger overall accuracy--efficiency trade-off.\
\
\\subsection\{Analysis\}\
\\label\{sec:analysis\}\
We further examine the gains in Table~\\ref\{tab:main-eval\}, asking whether\
\\textsc\{RUAR\} merely shortens reflection or selectively reduces low-utility and\
post-ready continuation. The utility categories used below are diagnostic labels\
for analysis, whereas \\textsc\{RUAR\} uses the utility estimate\
$u_j=p_j^+-p_j^-$ for advantage rescaling during training.\
\
\\paragraph\{Low-utility reflection after training.\}\
Table~\\ref\{tab:utility_composition_after_training\} applies the same\
answer-forcing utility diagnostic as\
Figure~\\ref\{fig:motivation_reflection_ambiguity\} to Qwen3-8B MATH500 traces from the evaluated policies. Compared with the base model, \\textsc\{RUAR\} produces much\
shorter responses and substantially fewer extracted reflective reasoning steps, while the helpful-step share nearly doubles from\
3.18\\% to 6.14\\% (+93.1\\% relative). More importantly, compared with the\
length-only TLMRE baseline, \\textsc\{RUAR\} produces shorter responses and fewer steps, but retains more helpful reflection: the number of\
helpful steps increases from 146 to 158, and the helpful-step share increases\
from 4.07\\% to 6.14\\% (+50.9\\% relative). These results suggest that\
\\textsc\{RUAR\} does not uniformly suppress reflection; instead, it shifts the\
policy toward denser high-utility reflection while reducing repeated\
low-utility continuation. Full utility-category details, including redundant,\
ineffective, harmful, and \\textit\{Alternatively\} token counts, are provided in\
Appendix~\\ref\{app:utility_composition_diagnostic\}.\
