\\paragraph\{Post-ready continuation reduction.\}\
Table~\\ref\{tab:pre_post_token_decomp\} decomposes the reasoning segment\
$\\bm\{y\}_r$ defined in Section~\\ref\{subsec:reflection_steps\} into pre-ready and\
post-ready regions using the detected answer-ready step. The base model and\
PRIME spend a large fraction of their reasoning budget after becoming\
answer-ready, indicating substantial redundant continuation. TLMRE reduces\
post-ready continuation through length-oriented training, but \\textsc\{RUAR\}\
further reduces it, producing the fewest post-ready tokens and the lowest\
post-ready share among the compared methods. This empirically supports the\
motivation of overthinking-mitigating rescaling: \\textsc\{RUAR\}'s token reduction\
is concentrated in the continuation regime after the model has already become\
answer-ready, rather than arising from uniform shortening alone.\
Appendix~\\ref\{app:pre_post_utility_breakdown\} confirms that post-ready\
reflective steps are mostly low-utility, and\
Appendix~\\ref\{app:case_redundant_continuation\} provides a qualitative example.\
